==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ḱerd-|*dʰeh₁-}}
{{bor+|en|fr|crédibilité}}, from {{der|en|la-med|credibilitas}}, from {{der|en|la|credibilis}}. {{surf|en|credible|-ity}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kɹɛd.əˈbɪ.ɫɪ.ti/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-credibility.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[reputation]] [[impacting]] one's [[ability]] to be [[believed]].
#: {{ux|en|After weeks of [[blow smoke|blowing smoke]], her '''credibility''' with me was next to nil.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Dylan Byers|title=Does anyone in Trump's White House have credibility?|magazine=w:CNN Business|url=https://money.cnn.com/2017/05/16/media/white-house-crediblity-gap/index.html|date=2017-05-16|passage=The inability of Trump's own spokespeople to speak authoritatively on the president's thinking and actions -- a challenge even Trump himself has acknowledged -- has left the White House with an enormous '''credibility''' gap, and has tarnished the reputations of many of its most visible spokespeople.|accessdate=16 May 2025|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250322064600/https://money.cnn.com/2017/05/16/media/white-house-crediblity-gap/index.html|archivedate=22 March 2025}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2022-01-26|title=Network News: DfT awaits verdict on COVID 'partygate' scandal|journal=RAIL|issue=949|page=6|text=The 'partygate' controversy has played a major part in undermining the '''credibility''' of Boris Johnson and his Government and has led to calls from senior MPs for him to resign.}}
# {{lb|en|legal}} A [[believability]] of statements by a [[witness]], as measured by whether the [[testimony]] is [[probable]] or [[improbable]] when [[judge]]d by common experience.

